course: dictionary
lesson: 7973
title: Recognisable and what it tells you
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Recognisable means easy to know or identify because you have seen or heard it before. You can recognise a friend's voice on the phone, or a song after just two notes.
explain: Read this context: «Abstract art does not show recognisable things; some artists painted one colour or simple shapes on purpose.» The artists did not paint things you could name, like a dog, a tree or a face. So recognisable must mean things you can easily identify.
explain: Check it by swapping your meaning in: «Abstract art does not show things you can easily identify.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. You may also see «instantly recognisable», which means you know it at once, and «barely recognisable», which means so changed you can hardly tell what it is.
fact: Recognisable is an adjective, and it means easy to know or identify.
fact: The verb is recognise, and the noun is recognition.
fact: In American English the word is spelled recognizable, with a z.
fact: Recognisable can also mean clear enough to be identified, such as a shape you can just make out in the fog.
fact: Uniforms and hi-vis vests make people recognisable, so others can spot them quickly.
exercise: «Abstract art does not show recognisable things.» What does recognisable mean here? | Hidden inside a box | Made only of paint | *Easy to identify, such as a dog or a face | Very large and bright
exercise: Why do people wear hi-vis vests on the road? | To look smart at parties | Because the vests are cheap | To keep warm in winter | *So drivers can see them clearly and recognise them
exercise: Which phrase means you know something straight away? | barely recognisable | hidden from view | *instantly recognisable | very heavy
test: Which word is closest in meaning to recognisable? | heavy | *identifiable | shiny | invisible
test: «The old photo was barely recognisable.» What does this mean? | It had been taken before | It was very loud | Everyone in it was smiling | *It had changed so much you could hardly tell who was in it
exam: Which sentence uses recognisable correctly? | The recognisable lunch was eaten quickly. | *Everyone knew the tune, because it was instantly recognisable. | She ran recognisable across the field. | The rain was recognisable on Tuesday.
